 (r"Each part code is reduced to 16 principal components fitted on the 165 fitting nouns. Ridge readouts take the penalty from 17 values between $10^{-3}$ and $10^{5}$ that maximizes $R^2$ on the validation nouns, so the penalty, like the layer and code, is chosen on nouns the readout was not fitted on. The random-weight control loads each architecture and tokenizer with freshly initialized weights (seed 0) and runs the same protocol.",
  r"Each part code is reduced to 16 principal components fitted on the 165 fitting nouns, and each component is divided by its standard deviation on those nouns. In the token-count variant, a quadratic in the number of the noun's tokens is regressed out of the code, fitted on the fitting nouns, before the components are computed. Ridge readouts take the penalty from 25 values between $10^{-3}$ and $10^{9}$ that maximizes $R^2$ on the validation nouns, so the penalty, like the layer and code, is chosen on nouns the readout was not fitted on; no penalty selected for a trained model lies at the edge of the grid. The random-weight control loads each architecture and tokenizer with freshly initialized weights (seed 0) and runs the same protocol, once with its own part codes and once with the part codes, components, and references of the trained model. The residual $\delta$ is the share of state variance that an affine map of the two whitened part codes does not predict on fresh pairs; the concatenation reference itself has $\delta$ between 0.04 and 0.81, because 16 components omit part of each code, so the verdicts rest on $\kappa$. In an earlier run with unwhitened codes, selecting the penalty on composites instead of nouns, as in a standard probe, fitted the fitting nouns more closely and transferred worse."),
